            {
              "text": "When acting as an executor in Queensland, obtaining a Grant of Probate through the Supreme Court of Queensland and administering an estate involves strict statutory duties, notice requirements (such as advertising in the *Queensland Law Reporter*), and managing potential executor liability. \n\nSeveral law firms with physical offices in or directly servicing **Chermside** and **North Lakes** have dedicated succession and estate administration practices:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Firms in or Near Chermside\n\n*   **Pennisi Zia Lawyers (Chermside)**\n    *   **Location:** Gympie Road, Chermside.\n    *   **Focus:** A long-established local Northside practice with substantial experience in Queensland wills, probate applications, estate asset collection, and distribution. They frequently offer fixed-fee arrangements for uncontested probate applications.\n*   **Vicca Law (Aspley — adjacent to Chermside)**\n    *   **Location:** Aspley.\n    *   **Focus:** A boutique firm with a strong focus on succession law, estate administration, and estate litigation. They assist personal representatives with probate, complex Letters of Administration, and defending against family provision claims.\n*   **Kelly Lawyers (Brisbane Northside)**\n    *   **Focus:** Regularly services Chermside, Kedron, and neighbouring suburbs for deceased estate administration, Grants of Probate, deeds of family arrangement, and advising executors on their legal duties to avoid personal liability.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Firms in North Lakes\n\n*   **Cooke & Hutchinson Lawyers (North Lakes)**\n    *   **Location:** North Lakes Central (Endeavour Blvd).\n    *   **Focus:** One of the most established legal presences in the Moreton Bay/North Lakes area. Their wills and estates division handles the entire probate lifecycle, transmission applications for real property, and complex beneficiary distributions.\n*   **Zande Law (North Lakes)**\n    *   **Location:** Discovery Drive / North Lakes.\n    *   **Focus:** A long-standing firm in North Lakes that frequently represents executors in probate and estate administration matters, particularly where family property and asset transfers are involved.\n*   **Ascendia Lawyers (North Lakes / Sunshine Coast)**\n    *   **Location:** North Lakes office.\n    *   **Focus:** Especially advantageous for estates involving complex assets, family trusts, or superannuation, as they combine legal estate administration with in-house accounting and tax services to streamline date-of-death and estate tax returns.\n*   **Cornerstone Law Offices (North Lakes)**\n    *   **Location:** North Lakes.\n    *   **Focus:** Handles standard and contested deceased estate administration, applications for probate, and Letters of Administration where someone has died intestate (without a will).\n\n---\n\n### 3. Queensland Specialist Boutique Firms (Servicing Northside)\n\nIf the estate is contentious, involves substantial commercial assets, or requires boutique succession counsel, consider dedicated succession specialists:\n\n*   **The Estate Lawyers:** A Queensland firm practicing exclusively in deceased estates, probate applications, and succession law.\n*   **de Groots Wills & Estate Lawyers:** A well-known Brisbane-based specialist firm dealing solely with estate planning, estate administration, and estate litigation.\n\n---\n\n### Practical Questions to Ask Before Engaging a Firm\n\n1.  **Fee Structure (Fixed Fee vs. Hourly Rate):** Ask whether they offer a **fixed fee** for the Grant of Probate application. A grant follows a standard procedural path, so many firms provide fixed quotes for drafting court documents, publishing required statutory notices, and lodging with the Supreme Court.\n2.  **Scope of Service:** Clarify whether you want:\n    *   *Grant only:* The firm handles the Supreme Court probate application, after which you collect assets and distribute them yourself; or\n    *   *Full administration:* The firm collects bank funds, sells or transfers real estate (via Queensland Titles Registry transmissions), pays debts, and completes final distributions to beneficiaries.\n3.  **Notice Periods & Risk:** Queensland legislation allows beneficiaries/claimants **6 months** from the date of death to give notice of an intended family provision claim and **9 months** to commence proceedings. Ensure the lawyer outlines when it is legally safe to distribute funds so you do not risk personal executor liability.",
              "thoughtSignature": ""
            }
